Track 2: Sluja-Baiting the Gale
- Story & Context: Inside the bunker, Petro confessed to the collective that he had been "Sluja-baiting"—luring the lightning to strike specific wires to force the radio station to manifest. He stood amidst a sea of tangled cables, the smell of ozone clinging to his wet wool coat like a second skin. He realized too late that the station wasn't a broadcast, but a hungry organism consuming the electricity of their valley. "Will the voltage remember my name when the wires go cold?" he asked, his voice cracking into a jagged, synthesised trill.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A gritty, close-up interior shot of a vintage radio dial glowing with blue electricity, reflections of an anxious, hooded figure visible in the cracked glass.

Track 3: Zenith-Fall Requiem
- Story & Context: The storm finally cleared, leaving behind only the silence of the aftermath and a radio that would never hum again. At the center of the ruins, a local shepherd found a pile of melted copper and a scrap of paper with the final frequency etched into it. No one ever found Marja, but the hills hummed with a lingering, metallic reverb whenever the humidity hit ninety percent. "Gone where the air turns to gold and the signal is skin," the wind whispered through the abandoned tower, a ghostly reminder of the last transmission.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A cinematic, melancholic landscape shot of a rusted, half-melted radio tower leaning over a field of tall, wet grass after the rain, a single bright beam of light cutting through grey clouds.

